  ],
  "text": "The deepest hole humans ever dug went twelve kilometres down, and then stopped. Twelve kilometres sounds enormous. You could drive it in ten minutes. Below that drill bit, the crust simply keeps going. Then the mantle. Nearly three thousand kilometres of it. And the mantle is not lava. It is solid rock that flows. About as fast as your fingernails grow. Given millions of years, that is enough. Deeper still, the metal turns liquid and starts moving. And at the very centre, iron so squeezed it stays solid at five thousand degrees. Shrink all of it to the size of an apple, and the crust is thinner than the peel. Every city, every road, every person who ever lived, on that peel. And that peel is cracked into pieces that are moving right now. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
